::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-110"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The assertion requires `logs[0].Message` to match **both** `ParseErrorMessageRegexOld` ("Refused to frame ... because an ancestor violates") **and** `ParseErrorMessageRegexNew` ("Framing ... violates") at the same time. These are two different browser-side CSP violation wordings for the same scenario — a single log entry will be in one format or the other, never both. Using `&&` means the `Assert.True` condition is always false and the test always fails with the custom message.

The intent is stated right there in the failure message: "Expected log message to match **one of** the CSP error patterns ... **or** ...". The operator must be `||`.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-110"}
        Assert.True(
            ParseErrorMessageRegexOld.IsMatch(logs[0].Message) ||
            ParseErrorMessageRegexNew.IsMatch(logs[0].Message),
            $"Expected log message to match one of the CSP error patterns: {ParseErrorMessageRegexOld} or {ParseErrorMessageRegexNew}. Actual: {logs[0].Message}");
